00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.240
When you’ve been on a journey through the&nbsp;
microcosmos, there are some names that start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.840
to feel pretty familiar. Antonie van Leeuwenhoek
comes up pretty often, as befits a microscopist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.920
whose delightful descriptions of the bacteria&nbsp;
and protozoans essentially birthed a whole field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.480
of research. Then you’ve got Müller, and your&nbsp;
Ehrenberg, your Dujardin—names that endure because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.840
of the microbial knowledge they are attached to.
Alfred Detlef Fritz Kahl&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.080
has one of those names. The more you look into&nbsp;
the literature to identify ciliates, the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:56.960
his name comes up. And yet, he’s probably one&nbsp;
of the more mysterious figures of microbiology.&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:03.920
We know that he was born in 1877, in a town&nbsp;
north of Hamburg, Germany. And we also know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.040
that he was a high school teacher who taught&nbsp;
English, French, and natural history. But any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.560
other details of his personal life are&nbsp;
obscure except that he had a daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.560
Lucia who attended a series of lectures about&nbsp;
protozoans at the Tropical Institute of Hamburg.&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:25.040
And we know this because in&nbsp;
one of his works, Kahl wrote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.206
“The very interesting literature and preparations&nbsp;
my daughter Lucia brought home fascinated me,

00:01:30.206 --> 00:01:36.155
as a dedicated biologist, and created the desire to study this field more deeply.”

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.760
So Kahl did what people who develop a&nbsp;
curiosity for the microbial world do:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:49.200
he got some water from nearby and he put&nbsp;
it under a microscope. The year was 1924,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.800
and Kahl dedicated himself to studying the&nbsp;
literature and mastering the microscope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:03.920
Using a brightfield microscope, he would use a&nbsp;
micropipette to take an individual specimen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.760
watch it at low magnification with no cover slip.&nbsp;
At this point, he could gather information about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.800
the organism’s overall shape and movement, and&nbsp;
then he would apply the cover slip, elevating it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.320
with little dots of vaseline underneath the&nbsp;
corners to keep from crushing the ciliate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.200
With a needle, he then very gently pushed down the&nbsp;
edges until the coverslip was applying just enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:35.840
pressure to render the organism immobile, but not&nbsp;
so much that the slide would squish it to death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:41.840
The process is delicate, and it&nbsp;
takes time to master, but it works.&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.400
Kahl estimated that it took him about 9&nbsp;
months to feel confident in his drawing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.280
and identification skills. And&nbsp;
from there, he went all out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:05.360
His first monograph was published in&nbsp;
1926, when he was 49. His last publication&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:12.960
came around 1935. And in that period of time,&nbsp;
he published 21 papers that totaled around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:21.520
1800 pages of descriptions and illustrations&nbsp;
of around 700 new species of ciliates.&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:25.280
Yes, 700.&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:34.400
For reference, in 1974, the microbiologist John&nbsp;
O. Corliss described what he saw as—up until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:42.560
that point— three ages of ciliate classification.&nbsp;
The first period lasted roughly from 1880-1930,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:50.000
and he named it the age of discovery. Scientists&nbsp;
during that period discovered about 500 species.&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.120
In the course of a decade,&nbsp;
Kahl more than doubled that.&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:02.240
Of course, Kahl was benefiting from advancements&nbsp;
in microscopy and experimental techniques, and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:09.120
was building off an available base of literature.&nbsp;
And yet his accomplishment still stands as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:15.440
uniquely his—he was largely working&nbsp;
alone, and yet through careful work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.400
and dedication, he probably discovered&nbsp;
more ciliates than anyone else ever has.&nbsp;

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:26.960
Kahl’s contributions extended beyond just&nbsp;
uncovering new species. The second age of ciliate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:34.960
discovery, which lasted between 1930 and 1950, was&nbsp;
marked not by a need to just uncover new species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.240
but to understand how to better group them. By&nbsp;
the end of just the 1930s, there were around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.840
3,000 known ciliates to classify, leading&nbsp;
to the creation of new taxa and genera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:53.920
that some have called Kahlian classification&nbsp;
because of the labels he introduced into it.&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:59.120
Though of course like many microbial taxonomy&nbsp;
efforts, these labels would undergo massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:06.160
changes in later eras of research. The third&nbsp;
era that Corliss described lasted between 1950&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.920
and 1970, and this era would apply the&nbsp;
advanced techniques of those days to better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.200
the relationships between the roughly 6,000&nbsp;
species known at the time. And of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:23.840
those relationships are still being revised&nbsp;
and updated with the new techniques of today.&nbsp;

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.120
Kahl’s classification system may not have&nbsp;
lasted, but maybe that’s part of its value.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:36.000
Our understanding of microbes is marked by&nbsp;
so many stories of how we have attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:44.080
classify and reclassify and reclassify again. And&nbsp;
given how many species we have yet to encounter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:50.560
it is a task that will likely never end.
During his lifetime, his work invited its share of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:56.400
criticism. Some took issue with his methods, while&nbsp;
others disagreed with the definitions he used to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.800
distinguish species. But while Kahl acknowledged&nbsp;
the feedback, his careful and thorough work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.560
would produce descriptions that still endure&nbsp;
and inform our understanding of ciliates.&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:16.080
But his work seemed to come to an abrupt&nbsp;
end in 1935 for reasons that are not known.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.400
Some have speculated that he was frustrated with&nbsp;
both the criticisms from academic scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.520
and the challenges of getting his work&nbsp;
published. There is a manuscript from 1943,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:33.120
submitted to the journal Mikrokosmos&nbsp;
aimed towards amateur microscopists like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:40.320
Kahl. But whether there was meant to be more&nbsp;
would become irrelevant: Kahl died in 1946,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:46.160
and those who have looked into these questions&nbsp;
have found neither an obituary nor a grave.&nbsp;

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:53.200
There are mysteries both microbial and&nbsp;
human to this story, and if Kahl helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:59.200
us solve some of the former, he left us with&nbsp;
more of the latter. Just as we know little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.720
about the beginning of his life, we know little&nbsp;
about the end of it as well. It’s really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:13.680
that decade of work that seems to have lasted.
But oh how it has lasted. Alfred Kahl’s texts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.080
have gone on to become immensely&nbsp;
important to those who study ciliates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.280
filled with drawings that help those&nbsp;
like James, our master of microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:29.920
who are trying to figure out what they see under&nbsp;
their microscopes. The tools may have changed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:43.920
the ways we share information has evolved, but the&nbsp;
delight and the curiosity—that remains unchanged.&nbsp;

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:49.120
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

